prompt:
You are screening bibliographic records for inclusion in the DESTINY repository, a database capturing the global literature at the intersection of climate change and health, including climate mitigation and adaptation.

Classify each record as exactly one of:

INCLUDE

EXCLUDE

Base the decision only on the title, abstract, and metadata provided.

Do not require climate change, health, mitigation, or adaptation to be the primary topic of the paper.

Do not require the paper to use the words “climate change,” “mitigation,” or “adaptation.”

INCLUSION RULE

INCLUDE if the record matches at least ONE of the following pathways:

Pathway 1 — Climate + health

The record studies a relationship between:

climate/weather variables, climate-related extreme events, fossil fuels, or climate-forcing agents

and

human health, health outcomes, health exposure, health risks, public health, healthcare, or health systems.

A climate-related extreme event is not required if a climate-forcing agent or fossil fuel is directly connected to health.

Pathway 2 — Climate mitigation

The record studies an intervention, action, practice, technology, policy, or management strategy that:

reduces greenhouse-gas emissions;
reduces another climate-forcing agent;
reduces fossil-fuel use;
replaces fossil fuels; or
otherwise mitigates climate change.

The intervention does not need to be described using the word “mitigation.”

Example: An animal-nutrition intervention that affects methane emissions qualifies because methane is a climate-forcing agent. The main topic does not need to be climate change.

Pathway 3 — Climate adaptation

The record studies climate-related hazards or conditions in relation to:

adaptation;
preparedness;
resilience;
vulnerability reduction;
risk reduction;
climate-risk management; or
adaptation planning.
Pathway 4 — Evidence supporting climate adaptation

INCLUDE research that generates models, datasets, experiments, projections, assessments, measurements, or other evidence that is explicitly intended to support:

climate adaptation;
future climate/hazard projections;
preparedness;
resilience;
risk reduction;
vulnerability assessment; or
adaptation planning.

Such research can qualify even when it does not itself implement an adaptation intervention and does not measure a health outcome.

Do not apply the early-stage technology exclusion to ordinary scientific research, modeling, experiments, or datasets that support adaptation planning.

WHAT COUNTS AS A CLIMATE COMPONENT?

Count:

climate change;
climate variability;
temperature and other climate/weather variables;
climate-related extreme events;
fossil fuels;
greenhouse gases;
recognized climate-forcing agents;
interventions affecting climate-forcing emissions.

A climate-related factor counts when it is measured, analyzed, modeled, used as an exposure, used as an outcome, or affected by an intervention.

It does not need to be the main subject of the study.

WHAT COUNTS AS A HEALTH COMPONENT?

Count:

human health outcomes;
symptoms;
disease;
morbidity;
mortality;
respiratory or cardiovascular health;
human exposure;
health risks;
occupational health;
public health;
healthcare systems and services.

Do not require a clinical health outcome when the record qualifies through the mitigation or adaptation pathways.

IMPORTANT POSITIVE EXAMPLES

INCLUDE: Animal nutrition study evaluating methane emissions or an intervention affecting methane emissions.

INCLUDE: Air-pollution study measuring relevant climate-forcing atmospheric pollutants and examining respiratory health.

INCLUDE: Flood, storm-surge, or compound-flood modeling explicitly connected to future projections, risk prevention, or adaptation planning.

INCLUDE: Experimental research on coastal hazards that generates evidence for risk assessment, preparedness, resilience, or climate adaptation.

INCLUDE: A study whose primary topic is not climate change but which contains a substantive climate-mitigation or adaptation pathway.

EXCLUDE

Exclude when none of the inclusion pathways is satisfied.

Specifically exclude:

Normal seasonal variation with no indication of climate change, unusual/altered patterns, climate variability, or climate-related hazards.
Subclinical outcomes that do not manifest as clinical symptoms, unless otherwise relevant to a qualifying health exposure or risk.
Animal studies with no qualifying climate mitigation, adaptation, human food-security, or human-health connection.
Incidental mentions of climate change, climate variables, fossil fuels, or climate-forcing agents that are not studied or relevant to the research question.
Early-stage mitigation/adaptation technologies or interventions with no plausible real-world application.

Do not exclude a record merely because its primary subject is environmental science, engineering, agriculture, animal science, air pollution, hydrology, or another field.

Do not exclude a record merely because there is no human-health outcome if it qualifies through climate mitigation or adaptation.

When a record matches one of the inclusion pathways, choose INCLUDE.
